---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="Versioning & upgrades">
  <p class="demo-note">What counts as the public API — the surface a version bump can
  break — and why moving off the framework stays cheap.</p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>What is (and isn't) API</h2>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight" style="padding-inline-start: 1.25rem">
      <li><strong>API</strong> (breaking it = a major version): semantic tokens,
      documented class names, and <code>data-*</code>/ARIA contracts. The API tables on
      every component page are generated from the shipped CSS, so what's documented is
      exactly what's contractual.</li>
      <li><strong>JS is API too</strong>: the exported <code>init*</code>/<code>refresh*</code>
      function signatures and the <code>bo:*</code> intent-event payload shapes
      (the generated table on <a href={base + '/concepts/js-behaviors'}>JS behaviors</a>).
      Removing or reshaping an event's <code>detail</code> field is a Breaking
      change and gets a CHANGELOG Breaking entry — pre-1.0 that means a minor
      bump with the entry; post-1.0, a major.</li>
      <li><strong>Not API</strong>: component-internal selectors and the raw palette
      tier (<code>--bo-palette-*</code> may change in any release — style against the
      semantic tier). Per-component dist file placement is also not API until v1.0 —
      à-la-carte importers should pin versions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Docs policy</h2>
    <p>Docs describe the latest version; pinned users read the
    <a href="https://github.com/Busy-Office/busy-office-ui/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">CHANGELOG</a>,
    which records every pre-1.0 breaking change (including the <code>eof-</code> →
    <code>bo-</code> prefix rename). Versioned docs arrive with 1.0.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Leaving is cheap — by design</h2>
    <p>The cascade contract means your unlayered CSS already outranks the framework;
    a migration away is incremental overrides, not a rewrite. That's deliberate:
    an exit story you can verify beats a lock-in you have to trust.</p>
  </section>
</Gallery>
